## Test Anti-Pattern Audit — `UserRepositoryTests.cs`

**Framework:** MSTest · **8 test methods** · **Production code:** `UserRepository.cs`

### Summary

| Severity | Count |
|----------|-------|
| Critical | 4 |
| Warning | 2 |
| Info | 2 |

Six of the eight tests have issues that could mask regressions. Only `GetAllUsers_ReturnsCorrectCount` is solidly written.

---

### 🔴 Critical — Tests giving false confidence right now

#### 1. No Assertion — `GetUser_NonExistentId_ReturnsNull` (line 20-25)

The test retrieves a user and does nothing with it. It will always pass regardless of what `GetById` returns.

```csharp
// ❌ Current
var user = repo.GetById(999);
// should be null          ← comment is not an assertion

// ✅ Fix
var user = repo.GetById(999);
Assert.IsNull(user);
```

#### 2. Self-Referential Assertion — `DeleteUser_RemovesFromRepository` (line 44-50)

`Assert.AreEqual(repo.Count, repo.Count)` compares the property to *itself* — it is a tautology that can never fail. The test would pass even if `Delete` were a no-op.

```csharp
// ❌ Current
repo.Delete(1);
Assert.AreEqual(repo.Count, repo.Count);

// ✅ Fix
repo.Delete(1);
Assert.AreEqual(0, repo.Count);
Assert.IsNull(repo.GetById(1));
```

#### 3. Swallowed Exception — `AddUser_DuplicateId_ThrowsException` (line 28-41)

The `try/catch` returns on *any* exception (even unexpected ones) and **silently passes when no exception is thrown** (execution falls off the end of the method). Both branches are green regardless of behavior.

```csharp
// ❌ Current
try
{
    repo.Add(new User(1, "Bob"));
}
catch (Exception)
{
    return; // test passes
}
// falls through here — test also passes!

// ✅ Fix
Assert.ThrowsException<InvalidOperationException>(
    () => repo.Add(new User(1, "Bob")));
```

#### 4. Shallow / Assertion-Free — `GetUser_ExistingId_ReturnsUser` (line 10-17)

`Assert.IsNotNull(user)` confirms *something* was returned but never checks it is the right user. If `GetById` returned `new User(99, "Wrong")`, this test still passes.

```csharp
// ❌ Current
Assert.IsNotNull(user);

// ✅ Fix
Assert.IsNotNull(user);
Assert.AreEqual(1, user.Id);
Assert.AreEqual("Alice", user.Name);
```

---

### 🟡 Warning — Likely to cause pain

#### 5. Broad Exception Type — `AddUser_NullUser_ThrowsArgumentNullException` (line 77-81)

Method name promises `ArgumentNullException`, but the assertion catches the base `Exception`. This would pass if `Add` threw `InvalidOperationException`, `NullReferenceException`, or anything else.

```csharp
// ❌ Current
Assert.ThrowsException<Exception>(() => repo.Add(null!));

// ✅ Fix
Assert.ThrowsException<ArgumentNullException>(() => repo.Add(null!));
```

#### 6. Negative Assertion — `UpdateUser_ChangesName` (line 53-60)

`Assert.IsTrue(user.Name != "Alice")` only checks the name *changed from* the old value. If `Update` set it to `""` or `"WRONG"`, the test still passes. Assert on the expected value.

```csharp
// ❌ Current
Assert.IsTrue(user.Name != "Alice");

// ✅ Fix
Assert.AreEqual("Alicia", user.Name);
```

---

### ℹ️ Info — Hygiene

#### 7. Print Debugging — `SearchUsers_ByPartialName_FindsMatches` (line 92)

Leftover `Console.WriteLine` adds noise to test output.

```csharp
// Remove this line:
Console.WriteLine($"Found {results.Count} users matching 'Ali'");
```

#### 8. Unused Static `HttpClient` — class field (line 8)

`private static readonly HttpClient _client = new()` is never used by any test. It's shared mutable static state and an undisposed resource. Delete it.

---

### ✅ Positive Observations

- **`GetAllUsers_ReturnsCorrectCount`** is well-structured: clear AAA, checks count *and* individual items, uses specific assertions.
- **`SearchUsers_ByPartialName_FindsMatches`** has a meaningful assertion on the result count (aside from the stray `Console.WriteLine`).
- Naming follows the `Method_Scenario_Expected` convention consistently.
- Each test creates its own `UserRepository`, so there is no shared-state ordering dependency.

---

### Fix Priority

1. **Immediate** — the 4 Critical findings are your prod-incident root cause. Any of these tests will pass even if the underlying code is completely broken.
2. **Soon** — the 2 Warnings weaken assertion specificity and could mask future regressions.
3. **Opportunistic** — the 2 Info items during next cleanup.
